mrbc bug: `mrbc -v`'s own parse-tree dump (mrb_parser_dump, parse.y) prints
garbage for a `$&`/`` $` ``/`$'`/`$+` (NODE_BACK_REF) or `$1`/`$2`/...
(NODE_NTH_REF) node instead of the real character/number, and can emit an
invalid UTF-8 byte sequence doing it -- a real, reproducible crash for any
tool (this project's own tools/bc2cpp/bc2cpp.rb among them) that reads that
disassembly as text.

Root cause: `node_to_int(x)` is `#define node_to_int(x) ((int)(intptr_t)(x))`
(node.h) -- correct only for the small set of AST nodes that stuff a plain
integer directly into a `node*` slot (mruby's own comment on that macro's
other call sites). NODE_BACK_REF/NODE_NTH_REF are not that: `new_back_ref`/
`new_nth_ref` (parse.y) allocate a real `struct mrb_ast_back_ref_node`/
`struct mrb_ast_nth_ref_node` and store the real value in that struct's own
`.type`/`.nth` field. `node_to_int(tree)` on one of these instead casts the
struct's own heap POINTER to an int and prints that -- effectively a
pointer's low byte, never the real stored value, and never deterministic
(depends on wherever the allocator happened to put it). `back_ref_node(tree)
->type` / `nth_ref_node(tree)->nth` (both existing accessor macros, node.h)
read the real field.

Reproduced directly: `lib/optcarrot/opt.rb:74` (3rd/optcarrot) has `body =
"if " + $1 + indent(2, $') + "end\n" if body =~ /\Areturn unless (.*)/` --
run any file containing that line through `mrbc -v -o /dev/null FILE`.
Unpatched, the `$'` case prints a raw non-UTF8 byte, and Ruby's own
`String#each_line`/`valid_encoding?` on that output raises `ArgumentError:
invalid byte sequence in UTF-8` (exactly bc2cpp.rb's own crash mode,
tools/bc2cpp/bc2cpp.rb:235's `each_line`). Patched, both `$1` and `$'`
print their real value and the output is valid UTF-8.

Debug-dump-only: `mrb_parser_dump` is never called from the actual
compiler/codegen path (dump_node's own case list under `mrb_parser_dump`,
called only from mrbc's own `-v`/debug tooling) -- this changes no compiled
bytecode, only what `mrbc -v`'s own text output shows for these two node
kinds. Verified against mruby's own full bundled mrbtest suite: identical
1874 OK / 0 KO before and after (same as patches/mruby-module-function-
scope.patch's own verification; see that patch's preamble for the one
environment-only "Crash" both runs share, unrelated to either patch).

Found scoping tools/optcarrot_probe (see its own README.md) against
optcarrot's real upstream source -- lib/optcarrot/opt.rb's own `$1 evens
$'` use (a `sub`/backreference idiom, unrelated to the `--opt` codegen
feature it lives inside) is what first hit this. Same patch-in-place
treatment as this project's other mruby-*.patch files, for the same reason
(no fork of upstream mruby/mruby this project controls).

diff --git a/mrbgems/mruby-compiler/core/parse.y b/mrbgems/mruby-compiler/core/parse.y
index 74727fe..fde3e21 100644
--- a/mrbgems/mruby-compiler/core/parse.y
+++ b/mrbgems/mruby-compiler/core/parse.y
@@ -8633,11 +8633,11 @@ dump_node(mrb_state *mrb, node *tree, int offset)
     break;

   case NODE_BACK_REF:
-    printf("NODE_BACK_REF: $%c\n", node_to_int(tree));
+    printf("NODE_BACK_REF: $%c\n", back_ref_node(tree)->type);
     break;

   case NODE_NTH_REF:
-    printf("NODE_NTH_REF: $%d\n", node_to_int(tree));
+    printf("NODE_NTH_REF: $%d\n", nth_ref_node(tree)->nth);
     break;

   case NODE_BLOCK_ARG:
